## Reuse generate-and-test on graphs

### What you will learn

Encode independence, coverage and domination over the same small graph.

### Find an independent pair

On a three-vertex path, an independent set of size two can contain only the endpoints. Edges are listed once; the constraint checks either selected endpoint through the body.

```elixir
result =
  Clingo.solve!(
    ~S"""
    vertex(1..3). edge(1,2). edge(2,3).
    2 { picked(V) : vertex(V) } 2.
    :- edge(U,V), picked(U), picked(V).
    #show picked/1.
    """,
    models: :all
  )

[%{shown: ["picked(1)", "picked(3)"]}] = result.models
Course.display(result)
```

### Change the property, keep the generator

A one-vertex cover must meet every edge. A one-vertex dominating set must reach every vertex, including itself. These are different definitions, though vertex 2 is the unique answer to both on this path.

```elixir
domain = "vertex(1..3). edge(1,2). edge(2,3). 1 { picked(V) : vertex(V) } 1. #show picked/1."
cover = Clingo.solve!(domain <> " :- edge(U,V), not picked(U), not picked(V).", models: :all)

dominating =
  Clingo.solve!(
    domain <>
      " adjacent(U,V) :- edge(U,V). adjacent(V,U) :- edge(U,V). reached(V) :- picked(V). reached(V) :- picked(U), adjacent(U,V). :- vertex(V), not reached(V).",
    models: :all
  )

[%{shown: ["picked(2)"]}] = cover.models
[%{shown: ["picked(2)"]}] = dominating.models
Course.display(cover)
```

### Try it yourself

Add edge(1,3). to the independent-pair example. Can two vertices still be picked?

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

The graph becomes a triangle. Every pair is adjacent, so the program becomes unsatisfiable. This is a valid result, not a parsing failure.

</details>

## Choose an exact hitting set

```elixir
workdir = Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/modeling", __DIR__))
```

Select elements so that every input set contains exactly one selected member. This is stricter than merely hitting every set. The input facts define the instance; the next editable cell is the encoding you must complete. Show solutions through `select/1`.

The starter is intentionally incomplete. A satisfiable run of that starter does not establish that it solves the exercise. The reference encoding later in the notebook provides a comparison once you have written your own rules.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "instances/instance-hitting.lp", ~S"""
set(1). element(1,a). element(1,b). element(1,c).
set(2). element(2,a). element(2,c). element(2,d).
set(3). element(3,b). element(3,c).
""")
```

### Complete the rules

Keep the input format and `select/1` output contract. Start with a rule that generates candidates, then exclude candidates that break the conditions above.

```elixir
~S"""
#show select/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/instance-hitting.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

The solve combines the instance file with your inline rules. `files:` supplies the instance; the string supplies the encoding.

### Compare with the reference

Alternative encoding: [hitting.lp](../assets/modeling/solutions/hitting.lp). [Test input](../assets/modeling/instances/instance-hitting.lp).

Compare your encoding with this readable reference. The collection files provide another formulation; compare projected model sets rather than enumeration order.

```elixir
~S"""
{ select(E) : element(_,E) }.
:- set(S), #count { E : element(S,E), select(E) } != 1.
#show select/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/instance-hitting.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

### Workspace cleanup

Save edited files before running cleanup. Rerun this section’s workspace cell to start again.

```elixir
File.rm_rf!(workdir)
:ok
```

## Choose nonadjacent vertices

```elixir
workdir = Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/modeling", __DIR__))
```

Choose at least the required number of vertices, with no input edge joining two chosen vertices. The input facts define the instance; the next editable cell is the encoding you must complete. Show solutions through `select/1`.

The starter is intentionally incomplete. A satisfiable run of that starter does not establish that it solves the exercise. The reference encoding later in the notebook provides a comparison once you have written your own rules.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "instances/instance-independent.lp", ~S"""
vertex(1). vertex(2). vertex(3). vertex(4). vertex(5). vertex(6).
edge(1,2). edge(1,3). edge(2,4). edge(3,5). edge(4,5). edge(4,6).
threshold(3).
""")
```

### Complete the rules

Keep the input format and `select/1` output contract. Start with a rule that generates candidates, then exclude candidates that break the conditions above.

```elixir
~S"""
#show select/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/instance-independent.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

The solve combines the instance file with your inline rules. `files:` supplies the instance; the string supplies the encoding.

### Compare with the reference

Alternative encoding: [independent.lp](../assets/modeling/solutions/independent.lp). [Test input](../assets/modeling/instances/instance-independent.lp).

Compare your encoding with this readable reference. The collection files provide another formulation; compare projected model sets rather than enumeration order.

```elixir
~S"""
T { select(V) : vertex(V) } :- threshold(T).
:- edge(U,V), select(U), select(V).
#show select/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/instance-independent.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

