## Reach every vertex from a selected set

```elixir
workdir = Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/modeling", __DIR__))
```

Choose a bounded set of vertices such that every vertex is selected or adjacent to a selected vertex. The input facts define the instance; the next editable cell is the encoding you must complete. Show solutions through `select/1`.

The starter is intentionally incomplete. A satisfiable run of that starter does not establish that it solves the exercise. The reference encoding later in the notebook provides a comparison once you have written your own rules.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "instances/instance-dominating.lp", ~S"""
vertex(1). vertex(2). vertex(3). vertex(4). vertex(5). vertex(6).
edge(1,2). edge(1,3). edge(2,4). edge(3,5). edge(4,5). edge(4,6).
threshold(2).
""")
```

### Complete the rules

Keep the input format and `select/1` output contract. Start with a rule that generates candidates, then exclude candidates that break the conditions above.

```elixir
~S"""
#show select/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/instance-dominating.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

The solve combines the instance file with your inline rules. `files:` supplies the instance; the string supplies the encoding.

### Compare with the reference

Alternative encoding: [dominating.lp](../assets/modeling/solutions/dominating.lp). [Test input](../assets/modeling/instances/instance-dominating.lp).

Compare your encoding with this readable reference. The collection files provide another formulation; compare projected model sets rather than enumeration order.

```elixir
~S"""
0 { select(V) : vertex(V) } T :- threshold(T).
near(V,V) :- vertex(V).
near(U,V) :- edge(U,V).
near(V,U) :- edge(U,V).
dominated(V) :- select(U), near(U,V).
:- vertex(V), not dominated(V).
#show select/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/instance-dominating.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

