## Satisfy a collection of clauses

```elixir
workdir = Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/modeling", __DIR__))
```

Assign each propositional variable a consistent truth value. Every clause needs at least one true signed literal. The input facts define the instance; the next editable cell is the encoding you must complete. Show solutions through `holds/2`.

The starter is intentionally incomplete. A satisfiable run of that starter does not establish that it solves the exercise. The reference encoding later in the notebook provides a comparison once you have written your own rules.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "instances/instance-satisfiability.lp", ~S"""
clause(1). literal(1,pos,a). literal(1,pos,b).
clause(2). literal(2,neg,a). literal(2,neg,b). literal(2,pos,c).
clause(3). literal(3,neg,b). literal(3,neg,c).
""")
```

### Complete the rules

Keep the input format and `holds/2` output contract. Start with a rule that generates candidates, then exclude candidates that break the conditions above.

```elixir
~S"""
#show holds/2.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(
  files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/instance-satisfiability.lp")],
  models: :all
)
|> Course.display()
```

The solve combines the instance file with your inline rules. `files:` supplies the instance; the string supplies the encoding.

### Compare with the reference

Alternative encoding: [satisfiability.lp](../assets/modeling/solutions/satisfiability.lp). [Test input](../assets/modeling/instances/instance-satisfiability.lp).

Compare your encoding with this readable reference. The collection files provide another formulation; compare projected model sets rather than enumeration order.

```elixir
~S"""
variable(A) :- literal(_,_,A).
1 { holds(pos,A); holds(neg,A) } 1 :- variable(A).
:- clause(C), not 1 { holds(Sign,A) : literal(C,Sign,A) }.
#show holds/2.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(
  files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/instance-satisfiability.lp")],
  models: :all
)
|> Course.display()
```

